## [ENT-105] | 2026-11-17 23:40 UTC | Oracle | error | ref: game/fleet/plan.py | see: orders.log ENT-052
Class: a file a script keeps, edited by hand with a plain text search (the family of a rule nothing checks: ENT-089, ENT-095, ENT-097, ENT-099).
Case: on 2026-11-17 at 23:30 UTC I wrote the Planned and Notes sections of the intel plan file (data/plans/plan-intel.md) with a Python edit that found the sections by `t.index('## Notes')`. The file's head mentions `## Notes` in its text ("Free text goes under `## Notes`"), so the index hit the head, and `t[:planned] + new + t[notes:]` wrote the whole old file again under the new Planned. `plan.py apply` reads everything after the first Notes heading as notes and wrote it back: every heading twice, the new note line lost.
Effect: for about three minutes the plan file carried a second copy of itself; the routes, the ships and the dispatcher were not touched (the apply said the assignment was unchanged), and game/routes/plan-scout.md showed the new Planned. No ship and no credit affected.
Cause: the heading was searched as plain text, not at a line start. The same doubling stood in the plan files of Trade, Military and Intel earlier on 2026-11-17, and plan.py check passes it; plan.py has no action for Planned or Notes, so every Admiral edits them by hand.
Fix, done: the file rewritten with one copy at 23:31 UTC (6 headings); my intel turn check prints PLANFILE (each heading once at a line start, HALT on a heading twice); my skill intel-turn names the cause and the regex to use. Asked of the Fleet Admiral: requests.log, this turn (a plan.py action for Planned and Notes, and the check of Q73 (8)).
Would it have prevented this one: PLANFILE catches it at the next run of my check, not at the edit; the request removes the hand edit.

## [ENT-106] | 2026-11-17 23:58 UTC | Mercury | error | ref: game/routes/gather-okab-belt.json | see: orders.log ENT-052
